<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jehoshaphat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jehoshaphat was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> in the first half of the 9th century BCE, the son of Asa. He made peace with Israel, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> and married his son Jehoram to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a> of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, and he fought beside Israel's kings against Aram and Moab. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> praises him for religious reforms and for appointing judges in the towns of Judah, and Edom remained subject to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jehoshaphat.jpg" alt="Jehoshaphat" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josaphat_rex.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Jehoshaphat was the fourth king of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, succeeding his father Asa; his mother was Azubah. Chronicles gives chapters 17 to 21 to his reign, and the First Book of Kings treats him more briefly. He ascended the throne at the age of thirty-five and reigned twenty-five years. He is said to have walked in the ways of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, spent his first years fortifying his kingdom against Israel and suppressed worship at the high places. In his third year he sent priests and Levites through the land to teach the Law.</p><p>His later reforms appear to have included judges in the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and a form of court of appeal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The Chronicler praises his reign for peace and prosperity, though modern commentary treats the figure of a vast army of 1,160,000 men and the rich tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> as not historical.</p><p>He allied himself with the northern kingdom: his son Jehoram married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab of Israel</a>. He nearly lost his life accompanying Ahab to the siege of Ramoth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilead">Gilead</a>, and the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>, son of Hanani, rebuked him for the alliance. He joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Jehoram of Israel</a> in a war against the Moabites, who were under tribute to Israel; the Moabites were subdued, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha">King Mesha</a> offered his own son as a sacrifice on the walls of Kir of Moab, the allies withdrew in horror.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chronicles also tells of a confederacy of Moabites and neighbouring peoples that marched against Jehoshaphat and camped at Ein Gedi. After the king prayed in the court of the Temple, the Levite Jahaziel announced that the host would be overthrown; the invaders quarrelled and killed one another. Jehoshaphat died soon after, at sixty, and his son Jehoram succeeded him. The medieval commentator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashi">Rashi</a> held that he died two years later and gave up his throne earlier. William F. Albright estimated his reign at 873-849 BCE. He appears in the genealogy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the Gospel of Matthew.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jehoshaphat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the time of <b>Jehoshaphat</b> the inhabitants of Mount Seir are said to have invaded Judea with Ammon and Moab.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Early wars were fought against the Kingdom of Israel, with which Judah alternated between conflict and alliance; <b>Jehoshaphat</b> fought beside Ahab of Israel, but Amaziah was defeated by Jehoash of Israel, who broke down part of the wall of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Military</a></li></ul></section>
